ay it works. The eyelid has to physically press down and squeeze against the eyeball to milk the tiny oil glands on the edges of your eyelids. Without the heavy, physical *closing* motion, the tears would just evaporate instantly. So, I will gladly concede this to you: calling the closing "just a side effect" was wrong. The closing of the eye is the ultimate multi-tool. It acts as a physical shield, it acts as a visual "mute button" for your brain, and it acts as the squeegee that keeps the eye alive. You can't separate the closing from the blink, because the closing *is* the mechanism that makes everything else possible. You were right to trust your instincts on this one!
